Corneal Ulcer
Opthalmology & ENT
Severe ocular pain, Visible corneal infiltrate/opacity, Hypopyon

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-016
Reason for Visit: Acute, unrelenting, throbbing ocular pain with rapidly declining vision and visible white spot on the eye.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified severe, acute ocular pain, extreme photophobia, and a gross visual defect in a contact lens wearer. Urgent physical assessment was absolutely required to definitively diagnose a suspected infectious keratitis (corneal ulcer), rule out endophthalmitis, and immediately initiate intensive topical antimicrobial therapy. Delaying evaluation until morning carried an unacceptable, high risk of rapid corneal melting, perforation, and irreversible permanent blindness.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "It feels like a hot poker in my eye, my vision has gone cloudy, and I can't even look at a lamp without screaming."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced 24 hours ago as a mild foreign body sensation after sleeping in soft contact lenses. Progressed rapidly over the last 12 hours into severe, unrelenting, throbbing right ocular pain. Endorses marked photophobia, uncontrollable reflex tearing, and profuse mucopurulent ocular discharge. Patient reports significantly decreased visual acuity directly proportional to a visible white spot on their cornea.
Functional Status: Incapacitated by severe pain and photophobia; keeping both eyes tightly shut.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living with roommate; highly anxious regarding permanent vision loss.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 102 | Temp: 37.6 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: In severe distress, wearing sunglasses indoors, utilizing a towel to catch profuse tearing.
Physical Exam: Visual acuity in the right eye is reduced to counting fingers. Marked reflex blepharospasm and pronounced eyelid edema present. Pronounced conjunctival injection with a prominent, deep red ciliary flush (circumcorneal injection). Direct visualization reveals a dense, white, 3mm circular corneal stromal infiltrate (opacity) located paracentrally. A 1mm hypopyon (layering of white blood cells) is clearly visible in the dependent portion of the anterior chamber. Surrounding corneal edema yields a generalized hazy appearance. Left eye is quiet and normal.
Environmental Observations: Dirty contact lens case and expired solution bottles on the bathroom counter.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Infectious Corneal Ulcer (Keratitis) - Status: Acute, Severe
Differentials:

Corneal Abrasion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a dense, opaque white stromal infiltrate and a hypopyon; simple abrasions do not present with deep tissue opacification or anterior chamber purulence.

Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of profuse mucopurulent discharge and a focal white infiltrate, despite sharing the symptoms of severe pain and a ciliary flush.

Viral (Herpetic) Keratitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The rapid, fulminant progression with heavy purulent discharge and hypopyon formation, more characteristic of aggressive bacterial (e.g., Pseudomonas) infection linked to contact lens abuse.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Threat to independent living if vision lost)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a textbook, rapidly progressive infectious keratitis, an absolute ophthalmological emergency. Urgent face-to-face assessment successfully identified the critical triad of severe pain, stromal infiltrate, and hypopyon, immediately distinguishing this from a benign superficial abrasion. Given the extremely high risk of rapid corneal perforation associated with contact-lens-related bacterial ulcers (particularly Pseudomonas), immediate cessation of lens wear and urgent escalation to an ophthalmologist for corneal scraping and intensive, fortified topical antibiotics was required. Delaying intervention would have resulted in catastrophic, permanent structural damage to the globe overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered zero topical anesthetics (strictly contraindicated). Arranged immediate, urgent referral and transport to the on-call Ophthalmology service at the tertiary hospital for corneal scraping and admission for hourly fortified antibiotic drops.
Positive Behavioral Change: To establish a sterile baseline and prevent bilateral infection or reinfection, the patient must immediately gather all current contact lenses, cases, and open solution bottles and throw them in the outside bin. The physical act of discarding the contaminated vectors provides immediate psychological relief and enforces the absolute necessity of absolute ocular hygiene moving forward.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handed over directly to tertiary ophthalmology service.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is being transferred directly to the tertiary eye hospital. Advised patient not to rub the eye under any circumstances, as the thinned, infected cornea is at high risk of traumatic rupture.

